    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Medina was an oasis settlement called Yathrib, held by Jewish and then by Arab tribes, until Muhammad and his followers moved there in 622 and it became the base of the early Muslim community.</li><li>It was the capital of the first three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, lost that standing when Ali moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, and afterwards passed in turn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharifate of Mecca</a> and Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a>, begun by Muhammad, grew with each ruler's additions, and in the Saudi period most of the historic city was demolished and the mosque vastly enlarged.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a> began as an open space for prayer with a raised and covered pulpit, and its piazza is now shaded by 250 membrane umbrellas.</p></aside>
    </section>

