    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 9th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The oasis is inhabited, about 1,500 years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">622</span><span class="rf-text">Muhammad and his followers migrate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> to Yathrib, which becomes the base of the Muslim community</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">627</span><span class="rf-text">The Meccans besiege the city for a month and withdraw after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trench">Battle of the Trench</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">656</span><span class="rf-text">Uthman is assassinated in Medina, and Ali later moves the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1517</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> take Medina after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> conquers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1908</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> reaches Medina</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharifate of Mecca</a> takes the city after the siege that began in 1916</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1925</span><span class="rf-text">Saudi forces conquer Medina as part of the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabonidus.jpg" alt="Nabonidus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail,_stele_of_Nabonidus._Nabonidus_wears_the_traditional_dress_of_a_Babylonian_king,_holding_objects_symbolizing_justice_and_power.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medina lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, a strip about 200 km wide between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafud">Nafud</a> desert and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. It is a desert oasis on a flat plateau at the meeting of three valleys, Wadi al-Aql, Wadi al-Aqiq and Wadi al-Himdh, and the water of these valleys leaves large green areas amid a dry and mountainous region. Basalt and volcanic hills surround it, and Mount Uhud stands to the north.</p><p>The city has been inhabited at least 1,500 years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a>, approximately the 9th century BCE. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> it was called Yathrib. The name appears in an inscription found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> that belongs to the Babylonian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> of the 6th century BCE, in several later texts, and in verse 13 of Surah 33 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>. By the fourth century Arab tribes began to move in from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, and around the time of Muhammad three prominent Jewish tribes lived in the city: the Banu Qaynuqa, the Banu Qurayza and the Banu Nadir. Ibn Khordadbeh reported that, while the Persian Empire dominated the Hejaz, the Banu Qurayza served as tax collectors for the Persian shah.</p><p>Two further Arab tribes, the Aws and the Khazraj, arrived later. At first they were allied with the Jewish tribes who ruled the region, but they revolted and became independent.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque.jpg" alt="Prophet's Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madeena_masjid_nabavi_12122008230.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bluemangoa2z at Malayalam Wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The arrival of Muhammad and about 70 Meccan emigrants in 622 changed the religious and political landscape of the city completely, and in the ten years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a> Medina was the base from which Muhammad and the Muslim army attacked and were attacked. It was the capital of the rapidly expanding Muslim empire under the first three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman. After Ali moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, Medina became more a place of religious importance than of political power, and it saw little economic growth during and after his reign.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiyah</a>'s governors dug the spring of Ayn az-Zarqa and built underground ducts for irrigation, and dams were built in some of the wadis. An agricultural boom followed. After the Umayyads regained power in 692, Medina had a second period of great economic growth: trade improved, more people moved in and the banks of Wadi al-Aqiq turned green. Nuruddin Zengi ordered a new wall in 1162 to enclose the new urban areas outside the old one, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> supported the Sharif of Medina and cut the taxes paid by pilgrims. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> scholars such as Ibn Farhun and al-Sakhawi settled in the city.</p><p>In the second Ottoman period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a> had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, a power plant for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> railway built. The railway ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> to Medina and was built between 1904 and 1908, and within a decade the population reached 80,000.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jewish tribes ruled the region until the fifth century CE, and toward the end of that century the Aws and Khazraj took control. Most modern historians accept the claim of the Muslim sources that the Jewish tribes then became their clients, but William Montgomery Watt held that the Jewish population kept a measure of political independence. Muhammad was agreed upon as leader of the city after 622, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> held it from 622 to 660. Mu'awiyah I began the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, which held Medina from 660 to 749.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> followed. From 974 to 1151 the Sharifate of Medina was in a liaison with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, and from 1151 the city paid allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengids</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>, while remaining formally allied with the Abbasids. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> controlled the city, from 1254 to 1517.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1517, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> conquered Mamluk Egypt, and held it until 1805. The First Saudi State took it from 1805, and in 1811 the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a> retook it. His son Ibrahim governed it, and in 1840 Muhammad Ali handed it back to Ottoman command. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharifate of Mecca</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Hashemites</a> held it from 1919 to 1925, until Prince Mohammed bin Abdulaziz conquered it on 5 December 1925 and it became part of the Kingdom of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kaaba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaaba.jpg" alt="Kaaba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ka%27ba,_Great_Mosque_of_Mecca,_Saudi_Arabia_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Mortel, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Before Muhammad the people of Yathrib were divided between the Jewish tribes and the Aws and Khazraj. These two Arab tribes had been fighting for 120 years by the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a>, and they fought four wars in all. According to Ibn Ishaq, all parties agreed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-medina">Constitution of Medina</a> after 622, which committed them to mutual cooperation under Muhammad. Modern Western historians dispute its nature, and many hold that it may be a collage of different agreements. Other scholars, Western and Muslim, argue that it is possibly one of the oldest Islamic texts.</p><p>Muslims hold that Muhammad built the Quba Mosque, the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, on his arrival and later built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a>, which began as an open space for prayer beside his house and the houses of his wives. Muslims also hold that he was commanded at Masjid al-Qiblatayn to turn his direction of prayer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>. Muslims hold that a larger part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> was revealed in Medina than in the earlier Meccan period. The Baqi cemetery holds the graves of family members of Muhammad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> and scholars.</p><p>The medieval city formed an oval within a strong wall 9 to 12 m high with towers and four gates, of which the Bab al-Salam was remarked for its beauty. West and south of the wall lay suburbs of low houses, yards, gardens and plantations. The economy rested on the sale of dates and other farming, and in 1920 some 139 varieties of dates were grown in the area. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> schools and a Shia minority, the Nakhawila, live in and around the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr.jpg" alt="Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_dirham_of_Abd_Allah_ibn_al-Zubayr_690-91.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Not credited, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Aws and Khazraj were hostile to one another, and their last and bloodiest known battle, Bu'ath, was fought a few years before Muhammad arrived. Its outcome was inconclusive and the feud went on. In 625 Abu Sufyan led a Meccan force against Medina at Uhud. About 300 men under Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ibn Ubayy withdrew, Muhammad's force of 700 advanced, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> posted on a hill left their posts, which let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>'s cavalry attack. The people of Medina suffered heavy losses and Muhammad was injured.</p><p>In 627 Abu Sufyan led another force. On the advice of Salman al-Farsi a trench was dug on the northern side, and after a siege of a month the Meccans withdrew because of the harsh winter. Afterwards Muhammad marched against the Banu Qurayza, who had been accused of agreeing with Abu Sufyan to attack the defenders. They surrendered, and Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, their judge, ruled that all the men should be killed and the women and children enslaved. In 656 a party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> attacked Medina and assassinated Uthman in his home. In 682 the people swore allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a>, which led to eight years of economic distress.</p><p>In 1256 a fire in the storage burned the whole of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a>, and in 1258 lava from the Harrat Rahat threatened the city before it turned northward. Lightning struck the mosque in 1481. The Siege of Medina began on 10 June 1916, and the people faced food shortages, disease and mass emigration. Fakhri Pasha held out until his own men arrested him and the Sharifate took the city on 10 January 1919.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/quran"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quran.jpg" alt="Quran" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birmingham_Quran_manuscript_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medina is regarded as the second holiest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and is generally considered the cradle of Islamic culture and civilisation. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a> holds the tomb of Muhammad, and Abu Bakr and Umar are also buried there under the Green Dome, which began as a cupola built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a> in 1297. Observant Muslims usually visit the tomb at least once in a lifetime, although this is not obligatory. The city's names record its history: Yathrib, then Madinat an-Nabi, the City of the Prophet, and then the modern Madinah.</p><p>Under Saudi rule the city was expanded and the old walls were pulled down and replaced by three ring roads. Almost all of the historic city has been demolished, including the tombs at al-Baqi, which the authorities held to violate Islamic law. Critics claim that 300 historic sites linked to Muhammad, his family or companions have been lost in Medina and Mecca. There is no archaeology or architecture from the time of Muhammad except what remains of a few stone defensive towers. The rebuilt city is centred on the greatly enlarged Prophet's Mosque, and the sacred core of the old city is closed to non-Muslims.</p><p>Medina is today the capital of Medina Province and has roughly 1.5 million inhabitants. The King Fahd Complex, founded in 1985, prints most modern copies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Medina, an oasis in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> called Yathrib before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, became the base of the early Muslim community when Muhammad migrated there in 622, and was capital of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>. Later rulers added to its mosque; in the Saudi period most of the historic city was demolished.</em></p>

